**Verdict:** The F-VS1 at [$51,841](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28049659?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the correct natural entry. The D-VVS1 at [$53,614](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24468417?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) is the standout — rare situation where you get top color for less than mid-tier color. Between those two, D-VVS1 wins on paper and in the resale market. Natural buyers with no flexibility on color should start there.

> **Farzana on Natural 3.5ct:**
>
> Most people ask me whether to buy F or D at 3.5ct natural. My answer is to look at this table again. The [D-VVS1 at $53,614](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24468417?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) costs [$1,773 more](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/24468417?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) than the [F-VS1 at $51,841](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28049659?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational). That is not a meaningful price difference for a stone at this level. Buy the D.
>
> If you are asking whether natural 3.5ct is worth it at all when lab delivers the same 9.5mm stone for [$6,711](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/28472159?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) — that is a values question, not a quality question. Both stones are real diamonds. Only one is rare.

